ere in the last step the identity A13 was used again. Plugging equations A21-A22 back in eq. A20 gives: ∂2V ∂ηαβ∂ηγδ = DαβDγδV = X i<j Xij Rij,αRij,βRij,γRij,δ r2 ij + X i  X k̸=i Rik,αRik,β r2 ik ρ′ (rik)    X k̸=i Rik,αRik,β r2 ik ρ′ (rik)  , (A24) where Xij is deﬁned by eq. 27, and we have used the fact that the resulting expression deﬁnes a symmetric tensor with respect to α ↔β and γ ↔δ. Finally, when evaluated at zero strain (η = 0, R = r), the combination of equations A16, A18, A19 and A24 proves the relations 21 and 26-28. Appendix B: Equations of motion in the NVT ensemble In this appendix we list the equations of motion of the widely used Nose-Hoover-Chains method [26–28], which was used in the calculations performed in this work. We use a chain of M thermostats with coordinates ηj and momenta pηj. The equations of motions for the i = 1...N particles are: dri dt = pi mi , (B1) dpi dt = F i − pη1 Q1  pi. (B2) The equations of motion for the j = 1...M thermostat variables are given by: dηj dt = pηj Qj , (B3) dpηj dt = ( Gj −  pηj+1 Qj+1  pηj, j = 1...M −1 GM, j = M (B4) where the forces are: Gj =    P i p2 i mi −gkBT, j = 1 p2 ηj−1 Qj−1 −kBT, j = 2...M (B5) with the number of degrees of freedom: g = 3 (N −1) . (B6) The thermostat “masses” Qj can be written in terms of a thermostat relaxation timescale τT [28]: Qj = ( gkBTτ 2 T , j = 1 kBTτ 2 T , j = 2...M (B7) The non-Hamiltonian system B1-B4 has the conserved quantity [26, 28]: Econs = H + M X j=1 p2 ηj 2Qj + gkBTη1 + M X j=2 kBTηj, (B8) where H is the true Hamiltonian given by eq. 14. Appendix C: Equations of motion in the NPT ensemble In this appendix we list the equations of motion in the widely used Nose-Hoover-Chains method in the isothermal-isobaric (NPT) ensemble [22, 25–27], which was used in the calculations performed in this work. As in appendix B, we use a chain of M thermostats whose coordinates and momenta are ηj and pηj, which are cou- pled the the equations of motion of the N particles. In the NPT ensemble, the volume V = V (t) is treated as a dynamical variable via the generalized coordinate: ϵ (t) = 1 3 ln  V (t) V (0)  , (C1) whose momentum pϵ is coupled to an additional chain of M ′ thermostats with coordinates η′ j and momenta p′ ηj. The equations of motions for the i = 1...N particles are: dri dt = pi mi + pϵ W ri, (C2) dpi dt = F i −  1 + 3 g   pϵ W  pi − pη1 Q1  pi, (C3) The equations of motion for the barostat variables are: 12 dϵ dt = pϵ W , (C4) dpϵ dt = 3 (PinstV −PV ) + 3 g N X i=1 p2 i mi − pη′ 1 Q′ 1  pϵ, (C5) where P is the applied external pressure and the instan- taneous pressure is given by the virial theorem: PinstV = 1 3 X i p2 i mi + 1 3 X i<j Fijrij. (C6) The equations of motion for the j = 1...M particle- coupled thermostat variables are given by: dηj dt = pηj Qj , (C7) dpηj dt = ( Gj −  pηj+1 Qj+1  pηj, j = 1...M −1 GM, j = M (C8) where the forces are: Gj =    P i p2 i mi −gkBT, j = 1 p2 ηj−1 Qj−1 −kBT, j = 2...M (C9) The equations of motion for the j = 1...M ′ barostat- coupled thermostat variables are given by: dη′ j dt = pη′ j Q′ j , (C10) dpη′ j dt = ( G′ j −  pη′ j+1 Q′ j+1  pη′ j, j = 1...M ′ −1 G′ M ′, j = M ′ (C11) where the forces are: G′ j =    p2 ϵ W −kBT, j = 1 p2 η′ j−1 Q′ j−1 −kBT, j = 2...M ′ (C12) The particle-coupled thermostat masses Qj are given in terms of a thermostat relaxation timescale τT as in B7, while the barostat mass W and the barostat-coupled thermostat masses Q′ j are written in terms of a barostat relaxation timescale τp as [23, 28]: W = gkBTτ 2 p, (C13) Q′ j = kBTτ 2 p. (C14) The non-Hamiltonian system C2-C11 has the conserved quantity [22, 25, 26]: Econs = H + PV + p2 ϵ 2W + M X j=1 p2 ηj 2Qj + M ′ X j=1 p2 η′ j 2Q′ j + gkBTη1 + M X j=2 kBTηj + M ′ X j=1 kBTη′ j. (C15)",2105.13414v1,"cond-mat.mtrl-sci, cond-mat.stat-mech, physics.class-ph, physics.comp-ph",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2105.13414v1,,b'\xaeGpB',b'\xc3\xf5yB',copper; elastic constants; molecular dynamics,