m. A. 107 (2003) 3803–3811. [50] K.D. Nielson, A.C.T. Van Duin, J. Oxgaard, W.Q. Deng, W.A. Goddard, Development of the ReaxFF ",2102.00943v1,"cond-mat.mtrl-sci, physics.atm-clus",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2102.00943v1,,b'\x14\xaekB',b'\n\xd7vB',copper; elastic modulus; molecular dynamics,,,,,Successfully downloaded and stored,0,1,4328.6,7986,g_ntcu
11,Saxena S; Spinola M; Gupta P; Kochmann DM,"Saxena, Shashank; Spinola, Miguel; Gupta, Prateek; Kochmann, Dennis M.",,Finite-temperature surface elasticity of crystalline solids,2022,arXiv,,,,,,,10.1016/j.commatsci.2022.111511,https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.13028v1,"Surface energies and surface elasticity largely affect the mechanical response of nanostructures as well as the physical phenomenon associated with surfaces such as evaporation and adsorption. Studying surface energies at finite temperatures is therefore of immense interest for nanoscale applications. However, calculating surface energies and derived quantities from atomistic ensembles is usually limited to zero temperature or involve cumbersome thermodynamic integration techniques at finite temperature. Here, we illustrate a technique to identify the energy and elastic properties of surfaces of solids at non-zero temperature based on a Gaussian phase packets (GPP) approach (which in the isothermal limit coincides with a maximum-entropy formulation). Using this setup, we investigate the effect of temperature on the surface properties of different crystal faces for six pure metals -- copper, nickel, alumimum, iron, tungsten and vanadium -- thus covering both FCC and BCC lattice structures. While the obtained surface energies and stresses usually show a decreasing trend with increasing temperature, the elastic constants do not show such a consistent trend across the different materials and are quite sensitive to temperature changes. Validation is performed by comparing the obtained surface energy densities of selected BCC and FCC materials to those calculated via molecular dynamics.",,copper; dislocation; elastic constants; elastic moduli; elastic modulus; elastic properties; fcc; homogenization; molecular dynamics; molecular statics,Article,Final,All Open Access,arXiv,arxiv-2204.13028v1,"Finite-temperature surface elasticity of crystalline solids Shashank Saxenaa, Miguel Spinolaa, Prateek Guptab and Dennis M. Kochmanna,∗ aMechanics & Materials Lab, Department of Mechanical and Process Engineering, ETH Zürich, 8092 Zürich, Switzerland bDepartment of Applied Mechanics, Indian Institute of Technology Delhi, 110016, New Delhi, India A R T I C L E I N F O Keywords: Surface Energy Elasticity Statistical Mechanics Gaussian Phase Packet Molecular Dynamics Quasicontinuum Abstract Surface energies and surface elasticity largely aﬀect the mechanical response of nanostructures as well as the physical phenomenon associated with surfaces such as evaporation and adsorption. Studying surface energies at ﬁnite temperatures is therefore of immense interest for nanoscale applications. However, calculating surface energies and derived quantities from atomistic en- sembles is usually limited to zero temperature or involve cumbersome thermodynamic integra- tion techniques at ﬁnite temperature. Here, we illustrate a technique to identify the energy and elastic properties of surfaces of solids at non-zero temperature based on a Gaussian phase packets ( GPP) approach (which in the isothermal limit coincides with a maximum-entropy formulation). Using this setup, we investigate the eﬀect of temperature on the surface properties of diﬀerent crystal faces for six pure metals – copper, nickel, alumimum, iron, tungsten and vanadium – thus covering both FCC and BCC lattice structures. While the obtained surface energies and stresses usually show a decreasing trend with increasing temperature, the elastic constants do not show such a consistent trend across the diﬀerent materials and are quite sensitive to temper- ature changes. Validation is performed by comparing the obtained surface energy densities of selected BCC and FCC materials to those calculated via molecular dynamics. 1. Introduction Surfaces of solids are known to have an energy in excess to that of the bulk, which can be attributed to a lower coordination number of the surface atoms. The eﬀect of this surface energy is in proportion to the surface-to-volume ratio of a sample. Hence, although negligible in large-scale structures, surfaces may have a pronounced eﬀect, when it comes to understanding the mechanical properties of nano-scale structures. Experimental [12, 55] and numerical [2, 5, 24] ﬁndings have shown that the stiﬀness of mechanical structures tends to deviate from its known large-scale values as one or more sample dimensions approach the material length scale, which is the lattice parameter for crystalline solids. For example, the elastic response of plates with thickness approaching a few atomic planes is drastically diﬀerent than what that of thicker specimens. As shown by Zhou and Huang [57], such nanostructures can be either softer or stiﬀer compared to the bulk. Studying surface energies is of scientiﬁc interest for a wide range of physical phenomena associated with surfaces, going beyond mechanics. Size eﬀects have been demonstrated to aﬀect physical properties such as the melting point [40, 49] and the onset temperature of evaporation [33] for nanoparticles. Surface energy also plays an important role in the phase transformation of gold nanowires [11]. Moreover, the strong growth of nanotechnology and the use of nanoelectromechanical systems (NEMS) provide for a growing branch of engineering that requires a careful understanding of surface energetics. In a continuum setting, the eﬀect of surface energies can be captured by considering the presence of an inﬁnitely- thin deformable surface layer covering the bulk material [13], which is endowed with surface stresses and associated surface elastic constants. Important works in the development of continuum surface theories include those of Shuttle- worth [41] and Gurtin and Murdoch [19], which allowed for the calculation of surface stress and elasticity tensors from atomistics by calculating derivatives of the computed surface energies with respect to the applied strain. For amorphous materials, attempts to study surface stresses have been mainly experimental [37, 54] because of the diﬃculties in their numerical simulations owing to their microstructure without long-range order. These works utilize the contact angle measurements of glycerol drops on solid surfaces to ﬁnd the surface stress. For soft solids, surface stresses may dom- inate the elastic energy, leading to physical phenomena associated with elastocapillarity [45]. These eﬀects become ∗Corresponding author prgupta@am.iitd.ac.in (P. Gupta); dmk@ethz.ch (D.M. Kochmann) mm.ethz.ch (D.M. Kochmann) ORCID(s): 0000-0002-5242-9103 (S. Saxena); 0000-0002-5180-6149 (M. Spinola); 0000-0003-3666-0257 (P. Gupta); 0000-0002-9112-6615 (D.M. Kochmann) Saxena et al.: Preprint submitted to Elsevier Page 1 of 20 arXiv:2204.13028v1 [cond-mat.mtrl-sci] 27 Apr 2022 Finite-temperature surface elasticity of crystalline solids prominent below a critical length scale determined by the ratio of the surface stress to the elastic modulus. Common examples include surface stress-induced rounding of sharp surface proﬁles [20, 21], reduced indentation depths and contact radii compared to those determined by contact theories that neglect surface eﬀects [44], and higher fracture resistance due to crack tip blunting [23, 25]. Some of these experimentally measurable phenomena are in turn used to quantify surface stresses in soft solids. For crystalline solids, experimental works [30, 51] have related the free surface stresses to a lattice parameter contraction measured by electron diﬀraction techniques. In addition, surface energy has also been related to the heat of formation of metals [4]. Such experimental studies have been complemented by an abundance of computational investigations. These include, among others, ab initio calculations [36, 50], Monte-Carlo (MC) [9], Molecular Statics (MS) [38, 39], and Molecular Dynamics (MD) studies [14–16, 53]. Miller and Shenoy [31] showed that the relative diﬀerence between the mechanical properties of nanostructures and the corresponding bulk properties follows an inverse relation with the characteristic length scale of the nanostructure. They veriﬁed this observation with atomistic data for extension and bending of nanoplates and nanorods. Shenoy [39] demonstrated the eﬀect of surface relaxation on computationally obtained surface elastic constants and proposed an accurate (al- beit expensive) procedure for ﬁnding surface energies of FCC metals under imposed strains. Recently, Sievers et al. [42] reported a computational homogenisation technique to ﬁnd the surface elastic constants by establishing an energy equivalence between atomistic and continuum representative volume elements (RVEs) at zero temperature. It is important to note that the majority of computational studies involved MS simulations at zero temperature (0 K). While this is computationally convenient, it is far from physical reality and does not easily admit extensions to non-zero temperature. A ﬁnite-temperature estimation of the surface properties from MD has been accomplished [7, 15, 53] using the procedure of thermodynamic integration as illustrated by Freitas et al. [6], originally introduced by Frenkel and Ladd [8]. Although beneﬁcial, this approach is unfortunately rather cumbersome in practice, as will be discussed later. Further MD studies on ﬁnding the size-dependent elastic constants of nanowires and nanobeams at ﬁnite temperature exist (see, e.g., [10, 52, 56]), but these do not quantify the speciﬁc eﬀect of the surface alone and hence to not lend to a transition to the continuum theories of solids with surface energies. Our objective is an accurate and computationally inexpensive methodology for obtaining surface properties of crystalline solids at ﬁnite temperature. We follow the Gaussian Phase Packets (GPP) formalism recently introduced by Gupta et al. [18], which captures the eﬀect of thermal vibrations via a statistical-mechanics approach by considering a Gaussian distribution of atomic positions and momenta and ﬁnding the quasistatic equations of equilibrium for the distribution parameters at ﬁnite temperature. For isothermal conditions, this approach was shown to be equivalent to the ﬁnite-temperature maximum-entropy framework of Kulkarni et al. [22] and Venturini et al. [47] for long-term atomistic simulations. We validate our results for representative cases by comparison to MD data obtained using the Large-scale Atomic/Molecular Massively Parallel Simulator (LAMMPS) [46] and thermodynamic integration [6]. The remainder of this contribution is structured as follows. Section 2 brieﬂy summarizes the statistical GPP ap- proach, highlighting the key points relevant for this work. Section 3 describes the methodology for obtaining surface energies, surface stresses, and surface elastic constants along with simulation details. There, we also provide a brief summary of nonequilibrium thermodynamic integration [6], as used in LAMMPS. Next, Section 4 presents all nu- merical results in two parts. We ﬁrst demonstrate the validity of our methodology to obtain geometrically converged surface free energies for various FCC and BCC metals and surface orientations at ﬁnite temperatures with comparison to LAMMPS data (Subsection 4.1). Second, we present surface stresses and surface elastic constants for all materials studied (Subsection 4.2). Finally, Section 5 concludes this study and discusses future directions. 2. Gaussian Phase Packet formulation: a review Let us brieﬂy review the recently introduced Gaussian Phase Packet (GPP) formulation by Gupta et al. [18] to the extent necessary for subsequent discussions. While traditional MD is a robust and well established method to study nanoscale systems, it often suﬀers from long statistical convergence times at ﬁnite temperature. The GPP approach derives inspiration from statistical mechanics, where the thermodynamic quantities of interest (such as surface or bulk energies) can be linked to phase averages depending on the positions 풒= {풒푖(푡) ∶푖= 1, … , 푁} and momenta 풑= {풑푖(푡) ∶푖= 1, … , 푁} of all 푁atoms in an ensemble. Determining those parameters from the statistically averaged equations of motion is the main objective of the GPP approach. Following Ma et al. [27], the GPP formulation posits a multivariate Gaussian form of the probability distribution function, i.e., 푓(풛, 푡) = 1 (푡) exp [ −1 2(풛−̄ 풛(푡))푇횺−1(푡)(풛−̄ 풛(푡)) ] , (1) Saxena et al.: Preprint submitted to Elsevier Page 2 of 20 Finite-temperature surface elasticity of crystalline solids where 풛= (풑(푡), 풒(푡)) ∈ℝ6푁is a condensed representation of the phase-space coordinate,̄ 풛(푡) = ⟨풛⟩= ∫푓(풛, 푡)풛d풛 denotes the mean phase space coordinate, and 횺∈ℝ6푁×6푁is the covariance matrix of interatomic positions and momenta. (푡) is the partition function, deﬁned via ∫푓(풛, 푡)d푧= ⟨1⟩= 1, integrating over all of phase space. Inserting the above probability distribution into Liouville’s equation leads to a time evolution problem [18] and deﬁnes the equations of motion for the mean positions and momenta,̄ 풛, as well as for the statistical information contained in 횺. In the most general case, 횺is a fully populated matrix, whose oﬀ-diagonal terms play a crucial role in interatomic energy transfer. However, the resulting evolution equations to be solved for̄ 풛and 횺are numerically even stiﬀer than the traditional MD equations. Hence, interatomic independence (i.e., Σ푖푗= 0 for 푖≠푗) is assumed to solve for the equilibrium conﬁguration of a system, while the interatomic correlations responsible for nonequilibrium irreversible thermal transport must be modelled separately. Since our focus here is on the equilibrium conﬁgurations of strained or unstrained surfaces, we limit ourselves to independent Gaussian phase packets, which implies 푓푖(풛푖, 푡) = 1 푖(푡) exp [ −1 2(풛푖−̄ 풛푖(푡))푇횺−1 푖(푡)(풛푖−̄ 풛푖(푡)) ] such that 푓(풛, 푡) = 푁 ∏ 푖=1 푓푖(풛푖, 푡). (2) Further assuming a hyperspherical shape of the atomic distribution function 푓푖in six dimensions leads to vanishing correlations of positions and momenta in diﬀerent directions. The only non-zero terms thus remaining in the covariance matrix are (with tr(⋅) denoting the trace of a matrix) Ω푖= 1 3tr ( 횺(풑, 풑) 푖 ) , Σ푖= 1 3tr ( 횺(풒, 풒) 푖 ) , and 훽푖= 1 3tr ( 횺(풑, 풒) 푖 ) , (3) where we deﬁned the covariance matrix of the type 횺푖= ( 횺(풑,풑) 푖 횺(풑,풒) 푖 횺(풒,풑) 푖 횺(풒,풒) 푖 ) , (4) consisting of diagonal block matrices. Therefore, the set of parameters to be solved for each atom becomes (̄풑푖,̄ 풒푖, Ω푖, Σ푖, 훽푖 ). Their evolution over time is obtained from the phase-averaged equations of motion, which result from inserting the above into Liouville’s equation [18]: d̄풒푖 d푡= ⟨풑푖⟩ 푚푖 , d̄풑푖 d푡= ⟨F푖⟩, dΩ푖 d푡= ⟨F푖(풒) ⋅(풑−̄ 풑)⟩ 3 , dΣ푖 d푡= 2훽푖 푚푖 , d훽푖 d푡= Ω푖 푚푖 + ⟨F푖(풒) ⋅(풒−̄ 풒)⟩ 3 , (5) where 푭푖denotes the net force acting on atom 푖having mass 푚푖. In the quasistatic limit, mean momentā 풑푖and mean thermal momenta 훽푖vanish for every atom. Consequently, the evolution equation for Ω푖becomes an identity, and we are left with four scalar equations in the quasistatic limit: ⟨F푖⟩= 0 and Ω푖 푚푖 + ⟨F푖(풒) ⋅(풒−̄ 풒)⟩ 3 = 0, (6) whose solution is the set of average positions̄ 풒= {̄풒푖∶푖= 1, … , 푁} and position variances Σ = {Σ푖∶푖= 1, … , 푁} for all atoms in equilibrium. Importantly, this admits decoupling the phase dynamics of thermal vibrations from the slow mean motion of atoms, which is essential towards our objective of studying equilibrium properties at ﬁnite temperature. Rather than resolving atomic motion at the femtosecond level, this approach tracks the eﬀective atomic parameters (̄풑푖,̄ 풒푖, Ω푖, Σ푖, 훽푖 ) over time (at signiﬁcantly larger time scales than required for (풑푖, 풒푖 ) in MD) and, in the Saxena et al.: Preprint submitted to Elsevier Page 3 of 20 Finite-temperature surface elasticity of crystalline solids quasistatic limit, reduces to a set of equilibrium equations to be solved for the aforementioned eﬀective parameters. Information about the momentum variances Ω = {Ω푖∶푖= 1, … , 푁} is obtained from the type of thermody- namic process assumed to bring the system to equilibrium. The entropy of the system is obtained using Boltzmann’s expression, 푆= −푘퐵⟨ln 푓⟩= 푁 ∑ 푖=1 [ 푆0푖−3푘퐵ln ℎ+ 3푘퐵 2 ln(Ω푖Σ푖) ] , (7) where 푆0푖= 3푘퐵[1+ln(2휋)]−ln(푁!)∕푁, ℎis Planck’s constant, and 푘퐵Boltzmann’s constant. Therefore, to simulate, e.g., an isentropic process, condition Ω푖Σ푖= const. for each atom 푖complements Eqs. (6). In this work, we will focus on isothermal simulations, for which the condition Ω푖= 푚푘퐵푇holds for every atom 푖in a system at a constant temperature 푇. The system of equations in (6) can also be interpreted as stationarity conditions, aiming to ﬁnd the minimizer of the Helmholtz free energy of the system, deﬁned by (̄풒, Ω, Σ) = 퐸(̄풒, Ω, Σ) − 푁 ∑ 푖=1 Ω푖푆푖 푘퐵푚푖 , (8) where we introduced the internal energy of the system as the average total Hamiltonian 퐸(̄풒, 훀, Σ) = 푁 ∑ 푖=1 3Ω푖 2푚푖 + ⟨푉푖(풒)⟩. (9) Here, 푉(풒) is the total potential energy of the system, typically deﬁned via interatomic potentials (as in this study). Eq. (8) represents a convenient way to calculate the Helmholtz free energy from our GPP-based simulations, which is beneﬁcial since is the relevant thermodynamic potential of interest for ﬁnite-temperature calculations with imposed strains (and considerably harder to extract from MD, as explained in Section 3). For a detailed description of the relevant Legendre transforms of the internal energy corresponding to the natural control variables for the thermo- dynamic process, see, e.g., [1]. In the following, we refer to the Helmholtz free energy as ‘free energy’ for conciseness. 3. Methodology In this section, we introduce the procedures used to isolate surface free energies and derived quantities (such as surface stresses and elastic constants) from atomistic simulations, using both the GPP approach and MD with thermo- dynamic integration. We ﬁrst outline the general procedure and simulation domain and then discuss simulation details for each of the two approaches. 3.1. Slab conﬁguration and surface energy deﬁnitions The surface free energy density can be deﬁned as the excess free energy per unit surface area 퐴, acquired by an inﬁnite crystal when split along a plane. To simulate this scenario, we use a slab of dimensions 퐿푥× 퐿푦× 퐻with two free surfaces of area 퐴= 퐿푥퐿푦separated by a height 퐻in the 푧-direction (see Fig. 1). While the top and bottom surfaces are free, periodic boundary conditions are applied in the transverse 푥- and 푦-directions to simulate an inﬁnite slab. The reduced number of interatomic neighbors at the surfaces results in surface stresses, also referred as surface tension. The equilibrium state of such a slab is governed by the balance of those surface stresses by the stresses in the sub-surface atomic layers [43]. The excess free energy in the slab due to the existence of the free surfaces is retrieved by subtracting from the free energy of the slab the bulk free energy (i.e., the total energy of 푁atoms, all having the same energy found inside the bulk of an inﬁnite solid, where 푁equals the number of atoms in the slab). This excess energy stems from two contributions: (i) the excess energy of atoms near the surface due to lower coordination number, and (ii) the strain energy induced in the sub-surface layers of atoms due to the compressive stresses imposed by the surface. One can deﬁne two diﬀerent measures of the surface energy in order to capture one or both of these contributions as follows. If we subtract the bulk free energỹ 푏푢푙푘of an inﬁnite periodic crystal in its relaxed equilibrium state from the slab’s free energy (both having the same number of atoms), the obtained excess energy density, which we call̃ 휉, is a result of both contributions (i) and (ii). By contrast, to compute the surface free Saxena et al.: Preprint submitted to Elsevier Page 4 of 20 Finite-temperature surface elasticity of crystalline solids Figure 1: Simulation slab used to ﬁnd surface energies. Periodic boundary conditions are applied to the surface atoms on those faces perpendicular to the 푥- and 푦-directions, while atoms on the top and bottom faces (perpendicular to the 푧-direction) are free. energy resulting solely from contribution (i) (as for example in [39]), one must subtract the bulk energŷ 푏푢푙푘of an inﬁnite periodic crystal compressed along the 푥- and 푦-directions to impose transverse lattice spacings equal to those of the slab in equilibrium. We refer to this surface energy density aŝ 휉. This leads to two distinct deﬁnitions of free surface energy density:̃ 휉= 푠푙푎푏−푛̃ 푏푢푙푘 2퐴 and̂ 휉= 푠푙푎푏−푛̂ 푏푢푙푘 2퐴 , (10) where 푛is a factor that accounts for diﬀerences in numbers of atoms (if any) in the slab and the bulk. Physically, we expect̃ 휉≥̂ 휉, as onlỹ 휉accounts for the excess energy due to the compression of the inner layers. As mentioned before, the slab shown in Fig. 1 usually tends to compress in directions transverse to the surface due to tensile surface stresses found in the metals studied here. Intuitively, one may argue that the amount of compressive strains present in equilibrium decreases as the slab thickness 퐻increases, because the inner bulk energy tends to dominate with increasing 퐻. In the asymptotic limit 퐻→∞, the transverse compression becomes negligible, so that the excess slab energy is only due to the reduced coordination number of the near surface atoms and lim 퐻→∞(̃휉−̂ 휉) = 0 ⇒ lim 퐻→∞̃ 휉= lim 퐻→∞̂ 휉=̄ 휉. (11) Quantitȳ 휉is the series-converged value, which can be independently obtained by using the same slab geometry of Fig. 1 initialized with the lattice parameter of the relaxed bulk crystal at the temperature of interest and preventing any relaxation of 퐿푥and 퐿푦. If the slab is suﬃciently thick to avoid interactions between the two free surfaces, (10) can be used to eﬃciently compute the asymptotic valuē 휉from a ﬁnite-size slab. Results illustrating the convergence of̃ 휉 and̂ 휉tō 휉with increasing 퐻will be presented in Section 4.1. To compute surface stresses and elastic constants, we strain the slab in the 푥- and 푦-directions, considering the aforementioned asymptotic geometry as the unstrained reference state. We impose a homogeneous deformation gradi- ents of the form 푭= 푰+∇풖onto the reference state of the slab (풖denoting the 2D in-plane displacement ﬁeld). From the deformation gradient, we compute the inﬁnitesimal strain tensor as 휺= 1 2(∇풖+ [∇풖]T). To extract the surface free energy density landscape as a function of the inﬁnitesimal strain tensor components, we simultaneously consider a periodic bulk crystal initialized with the relaxed lattice spacing at the temperature of interest, strained in exactly the same manner as the slab, and allowed to relax only in the 푧-direction, while keeping 퐿푥and 퐿푦ﬁxed after each straining step. Subtracting the thus-obtained bulk free energy from the slab free energy at the same deformed state Saxena et al.: Preprint submitted to Elsevier Page 5 of 20 Finite-temperature surface elasticity of crystalline solids Surface Symmetry Group Number of Neighbors Material Potential (001) (011) (111) (001) (011) (111) FCC 4mm 2mm 6mm {4,4} {2,2} {6,0} Cu∗ Mishin et al. [32] Ni Sheng et al. [38] Al Liu et al. [26] BCC 4mm 2mm 3m {0,4} {4,2} {0,0} Fe∗ Chamati et al. [3] W Marinica et al. [29] V Olsson [35] Table 1 Diﬀerent material surfaces being investigated in this study with their respective interatomic potentials and crystallographic surface orientations. The given numbers of neighbors {푍∗ 푁푁, 푍∗ 푆푁푁} for each surface type represent the number of nearest (푍∗ 푁푁) and second nearest (푍∗ 푆푁푁) neighbors present for any atom on the surface, along with the symmetry group of each surface. (Asterisks indicate those materials for which results will be validated through LAMMPS simulations.) FCC BCC 퐿푥 퐿푦 퐻 퐿푥 퐿푦 퐻 퐻min 퐻max 퐻min 퐻max (001) 8푎 8푎 4푎 180푎 8푎 8푎 4푎 180푎 (011) 8푎 6 √ 2푎 3 √ 2푎 125 √ 2푎 8푎 6 √ 2푎 2 √ 2푎 130 √ 2푎 (111) 6 √ 2푎 3 √ 6푎 2 √ 3푎 104 √ 3푎 6 √ 2푎 4 √ 6푎 3 √ 3푎 10