ce free energy density landscapē 휉(휺), we strain the slab geometry (and the corresponding bulk) in the nine independent straining modes summarized in Table 3. Speciﬁcally, we apply deforma- tion gradients 푭= 푰+ 휖∇풖, where ∇풖takes one of the displacement gradient forms of Table 3 (cf. [42]) , and 휖is a scalar incremented in simulations from −0.0015 to 0.0015 in steps of 0.0001. Each deformation case is applied to the Saxena et al.: Preprint submitted to Elsevier Page 6 of 20 Finite-temperature surface elasticity of crystalline solids Simple Combinations Uniaxial 풆1 ⊗풆1 Biaxial Opposite 풆1 ⊗풆1 −풆2 ⊗풆2 풆2 ⊗풆2 Shear + Uniaxial 풆1 ⊗풆2 + 풆1 ⊗풆1 Biaxial 풆1 ⊗풆1 + 풆2 ⊗풆2 풆1 ⊗풆2 −풆1 ⊗풆1 풆1 ⊗풆2 + 풆2 ⊗풆2 Shear 풆1 ⊗풆2 풆1 ⊗풆2 + 풆2 ⊗풆2 Table 3 Displacement gradients ∇풖used in the deformation gradient 푭= 푰+ 휖∇풖for the diﬀerent load cases to approximate the free surface energy density landscape. 풆1 and 풆2 are orthogonal unit vectors in the 푥- and 푦-directions. slab, so its periodic faces in the 푥- and 푦-directions are also deformed according to the deformation gradient. For each material and surface orientation, we numerically map the energy landscapē 휉(휺) through a large set of simulations. The derivatives in (13) are computed by ﬁrst ﬁtting a fourth-order multivariate polynomial function to the surface free energy landscapē 휉(흐), followed by diﬀerentiation of that ﬁtting function. 3.3. MD simulation details To validate the surface free energies obtained from the GPP formulation, analogous MD simulations have been performed with the same interatomic potentials for FCC copper and BCC iron. We here provide a brief summary of the thermodynamic integration in LAMMPS, which we used to compute free energies from MD simulations. Ther- modynamic integration is a general name given to a class of methods to ﬁnd free energies in atomistic simulations. This technique can be used in both ab initio [28] or potential based MD simulations [8, 9, 15]. However, we elaborate here on its implementation within the framework of MD simulations. Starting from a known equilibrium state whose free energy is known a priori, a quasistatic reversible path is constructed, which takes the system from the known free energy state to an equilibrium state of interest (e.g., the relaxed equilibrium of a slab at ﬁnite temperature). The work done along this path is the diﬀerence between the free energies of the equilibrium state of interest and the known initial state. The choice of the initial state depends on the application of interest. For example, Grochola et al. [15] found the excess free energy of a surface by switching from a bulk Hamiltonian to a slab Hamiltonian. Note that one does not need to initially know the absolute free bulk energy here, because the surface free energy is the diﬀerence between the slab and bulk free energies. This process, however, must be repeated for every temperature of interest, thus implying signiﬁcant computational expenses and eﬀorts for the present study. As an alternative, Frolov and Mishin [9] used the quasi-harmonic crystal approximation at some ﬁxed temperature as the starting point and performed a path integration parameterized by temperature to obtain the surface free energy at other temperatures. This class of path integration (or thermodynamic integration) methods was ﬁrst introduced by Frenkel and Ladd [8]. The basic idea was to consider an Einstein approximation as the initial equilibrium state. Considering the system to be a canonical (푁푉푇) ensemble and the thermodynamic path parameterized by a variable 휆, the free energy is given as (푁, 푉, 푇; 휆) = −푘퐵푇ln푍(푁, 푉, 푇; 휆), (14) where 푍(푁, 푉, 푇; 휆) is the partition function, given by the phase average 푍(푁, 푉, 푇; 휆) = ∫ exp[−(풛; 휆)∕푘퐵푇] 푁!ℎ3푁 d풛, (15) where integration over all of phase space is implied, with being the total Hamiltonian. Suppose that 퐼and 퐹 denote, respectively, the initial (Einstein approximation) and ﬁnal Hamiltonians. Our switching path is parametrized by = (1 −휆)퐼+ 휆퐹, so that changing 휆from 0 to 1 marks the transition from the initial to the ﬁnal state. For ﬁxed values of 푁, 푉and 푇, (14) thus yields d= ⟨d d휆 ⟩ d휆= ⟨퐹−퐼⟩d휆. (16) In equilibrium thermodynamic integration methods, the phase average on the right-hand side of Eq. (16) is com- puted at discrete values of 휆, and the equation is integrated numerically. This requires an individual equilibrium Saxena et al.: Preprint submitted to Elsevier Page 7 of 20 Finite-temperature surface elasticity of crystalline solids simulation at each discrete value of 휆during a single temperature integration. Freitas et al. [6] have proposed a quicker procedure by smoothly varying 휆from 0 to 1 in a single non-equilibrium simulation. Since this process is not qua- sistatic, the work done, 푊퐼→퐹, includes an extra dissipative term. Assuming this dissipative contribution to be the same for the forward (i.e., initial to ﬁnal, or 퐼→퐹) and backward (퐹→퐼) processes, it can be eliminated by writing the actual work done as (푊퐼→퐹−푊퐹→퐼)∕2. LAMMPS implements this procedure using the ﬁx ti/spring command. It equilibrates the system for a speciﬁed time 푡푒푞in the ﬁnal state, switches to the initial state in a speciﬁed time 푡푠푤, equilibrates again for the same time and switches back to the ﬁnal state. This two-way switching allows to compute 푊퐹→퐼and 푊퐼→퐹and thus the desired actual free energy. The initial state (Einstein approximation) is a system of independent harmonic oscillators centered about their initial lattice positions 풒0 푖. The Hamiltonian of such a system is 퐼= 푁 ∑ 푖=1 (|풑푖|2 2푚푖 + 푘푖 2 |||풒푖−풒0 푖 ||| 2) . (17) Using this deﬁnition of the Hamiltonian in a canonical ensemble probability distribution and the relation 푘푖= 3푘퐵푇∕⟨|Δ풒|2⟩ (with Δ풒= 풒푖−풒0 푖) yields the independent GPP distribution function (2). Unlike the GPP approach, close estimates of the mean squared displacements are determined here using prior bulk simulations. These estimates deﬁne the starting state for performing the Frenkel-Ladd thermodynamic integration on our system. In a prior simulation, we obtain the relaxed bulk lattice parameters and mean-squared displacements ⟨|Δ풒|2⟩, using the isoenthalpic-isobaric (푁푃퐻) ensemble and a Langevin thermostat for ﬁve equispaced temperature levels from 100 K to 500 K. The thus-obtained lattice parameters are then used to create bulk and slab geometries. The slab is given the boundary conditions discussed in Section 3.1 (see Fig. 1), and it is allowed to relax under an 푁푉푇ensemble sampled with a Langevin thermostat. Large simulation cells with 20 × 20 × 20 unit cells are used to approach the thermodynamic limit and to ensure the convergence of thermodynamic averages with increasing system size. The bulk models have 16,000 and 32,000 atoms, respectively, for the BCC and FCC lattice types. While doing the Frenkel-Ladd path integration, the previously computed ⟨|Δ풒|2⟩values are used to ﬁnd the spring constants for the Einstein crystal approximation. Long equilibration times of 10 ns are used for the slabs to obtain accurate time averages, while the thermodynamic switching between the original system and the quasi-harmonic approximation is performed in 2 ns. 4. Results and Discussion In this section, we summarize the results of our GPP methodology for surface free energies, surface stresses, and elastic constants as functions of temperature. In Section 4.1 we begin by demonstrating convergence of the lattice spacing and the surface free energies̃ 휉and̂ 휉with increasing slab thickness. In the following we focus on the converged inﬁnite-thickness scenario, for which we report the eﬀect of temperature on the surface properties – in comparison with LAMMPS data for selected materials for validation. Having validated the GPP methodology, we present in Section 4.2 results for surface stresses and surface elastic constants vs. temperature. 4.1. Thickness convergence study In this Section, we verify the convergence of̃ 휉and̂ 휉with increasing slab thickness 퐻. Fig. 2 shows the convergence of the lattice parameter 푎in the 푥-푦-plane at 0 K and 300 K for copper and iron slabs (each normalized by the respective lattice parameter 푎0 in the bulk). In all cases, the lattice parameter converges to the correct bulk value, as expected. The convergence for copper is fastest for the (111) surface, while the values for iron converge fastest for the (011) surface. The (001) surface shows slowest convergence for both materials. As expected, the compression of the sub-surface atomic layers vanishes as the slab thickness approaches inﬁnity, leading to a uniform bulk spacing in the 푥-푦-plane. As expected, the surface lattice parameter increases with temperature (as does the bulk lattice parameter). The asymptotic convergence in Eq. (11) is visualized in Fig. 3, which plots the surface free energies for diﬀerent surface orientations of copper and iron at 300 K. For all surfaces, we consistently observẽ 휉>̂ 휉due to the additional strain energy of the sub-surface atomic layers. The convergence characteristics observed in Fig. 3 can be related to the lattice parameters of Fig. 2. For example, slab surfaces such as (011) and (111) in iron, which exhibit little compression of the sub-surface layers, show little diﬀerence betweeñ 휉and̂ 휉. The other surfaces show a signiﬁcant compression in the 푥-푦-plane and therefore considerable diﬀerences between the values of̃ 휉and̂ 휉for small slab thicknesses. Saxena et al.: Preprint submitted to Elsevier Page 8 of 20 Finite-temperature surface elasticity of crystalline solids 0 25 50 75 100 125 150 175 H/a0 0.985 0.990 0.995 1.000 1.005 a/a0 Cu 0 25 50 75 100 125 150 175 H/a0 0.980 0.985 0.990 0.995 1.000 1.005 a/a0 Fe 0K (001) 300K (001) 0K (011) 300K (011) 0K (111) 300K (111) Figure 2: Lattice parameter convergence with increasing thickness for copper and iron slabs at 0 K and 300 K (normalized by the respective bulk lattice parameter 푎0 of each material). The corresponding asymptotic values are shown as black solid lines. Fig. 4 illustrates the dependence of the surface free energy density on temperature for all studied FCC and BCC metal surfaces. (Table 4 lists the values of̄ 휉, as obtained from the GPP framework for every metal and surface orien- tation at four temperature levels.) A unanimous trend for all materials,̄ 휉decreases with temperature. For FCC metals we observē 휉(011) >̄ 휉(001) >̄ 휉(111), which can be attributed to the number of nearest and second-nearest neighbor bonds that are broken by creating the respective surface (cf. Table 1 for the number of nearest and second-nearest neighbors, 푍∗ 푁푁and 푍∗ 푆푁푁, of a surface atom). Although an FCC (001) surface has most neighbors available, the FCC (111) face has most nearest neighbors. This is why it has the least surface energy, followed by the (001) and ﬁnally (011) surface, the latter having the least number of neighbors. By contrast, the BCC (111) surface has neither a nearest nor a second-nearest neighbor on the surface. The (001) surface has only second-nearest neighbors and the (011) surface has both nearest and second-nearest neighbors on the surface. Therefore, the trend for BCC metals is̄ 휉(111) >̄ 휉(001) >̄ 휉(011). The values of̄ 휉obtained using thermodynamic integration within LAMMPS match closely with the reported data obtained from our GPP framework. Discrepancies between GPP and MD data increase with increasing temperature. This is expected since the quasi-harmonic assumption of independent Gaussian phase packets for every atom becomes a poorer approximation of the actual crystal due to stronger anharmonic interactions in lieu of more atomic vibrations at higher temperatures. However, the GPP results always stay within 2% of the LAMMPS values. This comparison serves as a validation for using the GPP-based approach to study surface free energies for the aforementioned temperature range – which is why we will use the GPP-based approach in the following for our results. We note that the Fe (111) surface, which is the least accurate with increasing temperature, is a special case, as it tends to show faceting at elevated temperature: surface atoms may hop out of the surface, creating vacancies, as shown by Grochola et al. [14], who used a speciﬁcally adapted thermodynamic integration technique. The usual Frenkel-Ladd path fails here because the atoms move too far from their initial positions, about which the Einstein solid approximation is made. Therefore, we report MD results for this surface only up to 400K. The shown results are also in good agreement with the MD ﬁnite temperature calculations for Fe by Xing et al. [53] and for Cu by Frolov and Mishin [9]. For reference, Fig. 4 also contains data from Density Functional Theory (DFT) for Fe by Schönecker et al. [36] and for Al by Wang et al. [50]. Although the DFT values come with an oﬀset from those reported here from MD and the GPP approach, their trends with temperature match very well. Note that we would not expect perfect agreement between ab-initio data and those from ﬁtted MD potentials. Most importantly, we see excellent agreement between MD and our GPP approach (using the Saxena et al.: Preprint submitted to Elsevier Page 9 of 20 Finite-temperature surface elasticity of crystalline solids 101 102 H/a0 3.02 3.03 3.04 3.05 3.06 ξa2 0/Ec × 10 Cu (001) 101 102 H/a0 3.31 3.32 3.33 3.34 3.35 Cu (011) 101 102 H/a0 2.790 2.795 2.800 2.805 2.810 Cu (111) 101 102 H/a0 2.86 2.88 2.90 2.92 2.94 ξa2 0/Ec × 10 Fe (001) 101 102 H/a0 2.738 2.740 2.742 2.744 2.746 Fe (011) 101 102 H/a0 3.094 3.098 3.102 3.106 Fe (111) ˜ξ ˆξ ¯ξ Figure 3: Convergence of the two surface free energy density measures̃ 휉and̂ 휉to the asymptotic valuē 휉with increasing slab thickness at 300 K. Slab thickness 퐻is normalized by the lattice spacing 푎0, while the surface energy density 휉is normalized by the zero-temperature cohesive energy 퐸푐. Cu Ni Al (001) (011) (111) (001) (011) (111) (001) (011) (111) 0 K 0.0840 0.0921 0.077