, we present the directional compliance 푆in a polar plot for diﬀerent surfaces. The directional compliance as a function of the direction 풅can be computed as 푆(풅) = 퐶−1 푖푗푘푙푑푖푑푗푑푘푑푙, where |풅| = 1 (18) and we use Einstein’s summation convention over repeated indices. The directional stiﬀness 퐸(풅) = 1∕푆(풅) is the inverse of 푆. However, since the surface elastic tensor 푪is not necessarily positive deﬁnite (as also emphasized by Shenoy [39]), the value of 푆may changes sign while traversing along the polar axis. As this creates large jumps in the directional stiﬀness, we rather plot 푆= 1∕퐸in the polar plots. We stress that a lack of positive-deﬁniteness in the surface elastic constants (unlike the bulk elastic constants) does not necessarily constitute a material instability. In fact, all slabs investigated here were stable under the given boundary conditions. This is due to the sub-surface atoms in the slab, whose potential energy may stabilize a surface with non-positive-deﬁnite elastic constants. It is interesting to note that this also leads to the surface’s directional Poisson’s ratio to lie outside the usual range of [−1, 0.5]. As only limited literature data is available for ﬁnite-temperature surface stresses and elastic constants in metals, we usually report comparisons of elastic constants for 0 K and, if available, for stresses at ﬁnite temperature. Saxena et al.: Preprint submitted to Elsevier Page 11 of 20 Finite-temperature surface elasticity of crystalline solids 0 100 200 300 400 500 T(K) 6 7 8 9 10 τavg × 102 (eV/˚A 2) Cu 0 100 200 300 400 500 T(K) 0.7 0.9 1.1 1.3 τavg × 10 (eV/˚A 2) Ni 0 100 200 300 400 500 T(K) 5 6 7 8 9 τavg × 102 (eV/˚A 2) Al GPP: (001) MD: Cu (001) GPP: (011) MD: Cu (011) GPP: (111) MD: Cu (111) Figure 5: Average surface stress vs. temperature for FCC metals (MD data for Cu, obtained via thermodynamic integration in LAMMPS, is included for comparison). 4.2.1. FCC metals Fig. 5 summarizes surface stresses of FCC metals vs. temperature. For Cu, we again observe a good general agreement between our GPP data and those obtained from thermodynamic integration via MD. A common decreasing trend is observed with increasing temperature for all surfaces, except for the (001) and (111) surfaces of Al. Ni displays a change in slope, initially stresses increase by roughly 0.7% for the (111) surface and by less than 0.2% for the other surfaces, before they start to decrease. The eﬀect of temperature on the surface stress is minimal for Ni and maximal for Al, which is expected since it is inversely proportional to the melting points of the metals. Although not shown here, the Monte-Carlo values obtained by Frolov and Mishin [9] for (011) Cu, using the same interatomic potential, match the GPP results to within 1% relative error. The overall trend for FCC metal surfaces is 휏avg (001) > 휏avg (011) > 휏avg (111) for Cu and Ni at all temperatures. For Al, the trend is 휏avg (011) > 휏avg (111) > 휏avg (001) below 300 K, above which 휏avg (111) > 휏avg (011). The 0 K trend for Al, as shown in [39] using the potential developed by Liu et al. [26], matches our trend, while using the potential by Voter et al. [48] shows a trend similar to Cu and Ni in our work. Fig. 6 shows the directional compliance for all surface orientations as eﬀective quarter or semi polar plots (detailed values of surface stresses and elastic constants are consolidated in Appendix A; note that minor symmetries 퐶푖푗푘푙= 퐶푗푖푘푙= 퐶푖푗푙푘hold, while major symmetries do not necessarily hold for surface elastic tensors). It is diﬃcult to deduce any general trends with temperature – even for the isotropic (111) surfaces. Yet, the following explanations can be given for surface stresses and associated elastic constants for each of the three surface orientations. The (001) surface in FCC metals is characterized by isotropic surface stresses but anisotropic elastic constants, as expected from the 4mm symmetry of this surface. This anisotropy is clearly visible by the non-circular contours in Fig. 6. Surface stresses for Cu at 0 K are higher by 8.8% than those obtained from ﬁrst-principle calculations by Gumbsch and Daw [17], and signiﬁcantly higher (by 44%) than the molecular statics results of Shenoy [39], who used the (older) EAM potential of Oh and Johnson [34]. The reported 퐶1111 = 퐶2222 values are negative, while 퐶1122 = 퐶2211 is positive for all metals, as also seen for 0 K results obtained by Shenoy [39]. At higher temperature, the 퐶1122 values become negative for Al. The (011) surface has a two-fold 2mm rotational symmetry and therefore diﬀerent values for 휏11 and 휏22. All studied FCC metals show a decreasing trend of the (011) average surface stress with temperature. Unlike for the potential used in Shenoy [39], we ﬁnd a positive surface stress for Ni at 0 K. Also, the relation 휏11 > 휏22 holds for all examined metals and temperatures. This is expected, as every surface atom has two nearest neighbors along the 푥-direction and second-nearest neighbors along the 푦-direction, resulting in a stronger inward surface contraction along the 푥-direction. All elastic constants are negative for this surface for all three metals. The 퐶1111 values are signiﬁcantly larger in magnitude than 퐶2222, while 퐶1122 and 퐶2211 are close to each other. The close-packed (111) plane of FCC metals is isotropic with a 6mm symmetry, so that the surface stresses and elastic constants are isotropic. The latter is veriﬁed by the identity 2퐶1212 = 퐶1111 −퐶1122. The only material seen to deviate from this trend is Al. Note that this ﬁnding diﬀers from Shenoy’s results for Al at 0 K, who used diﬀerent Saxena et al.: Preprint submitted to Elsevier Page 12 of 20 Finite-temperature surface elasticity of crystalline solids Figure 6: Polar compliance plots for FCC metals. Per the FCC surface symmetries, only one quarter/semi-circle of the full polar plot is shown for diﬀerent surface orientations, which can be rotated about the origin to visualize the full polar plots (see Table 1). All compliance values are in Å2∕푒푉. interatomic potentials. The surface stress generally decreases with temperature, again except for Al. 퐶1111 = 퐶2222 values are negative at all temperatures for Cu and Ni but positive for Al, as also seen in [39]. An interesting observation for Cu and Ni is that values for 퐶1111 and 퐶1122 are similar in magnitude but opposite in sign, which makes 퐶1212 also of similar magnitude by the above identity. Combined with the fact that these are isotropic surfaces, the above results in the surface’s Poisson’s ratio to be close to −1. 4.2.2. BCC Metals Fig. 7 shows the average surface stress as a function of temperature. Unlike for FCC metals, a general decreasing trend with temperature is absent here and the aﬀect of temperature is much less pronounced, especially for W and V. The (001) surface shows an increase in surface stress, while the (011) and (111) surfaces exhibit a decreasing surface stress with increasing temperature. Also, the (001) surface has a signiﬁcantly higher average surface stress compared to the other surfaces of the three materials. The general trend is 휏avg (001) > 휏avg (111) > 휏avg (011) for Fe and 휏avg (001) > 휏avg (011) > 휏avg (111) for W and V at all temperatures studied. Fig. 8 shows the polar compliance plots of the surface elastic constants of Fe, 0 100 200 300 400 500 T(K) 0.2 0.6 1.0 1.4 τavg × 10 (eV/˚A 2) Fe 0 100 200 300 400 500 T(K) 0.2 1.0 1.8 2.6 τavg × 10 (eV/˚A 2) W 0 100 200 300 400 500 T(K) 0.2 0.6 1.0 1.4 τavg × 10 (eV/˚A 2) V GPP: (001) MD: Fe(001) GPP: (011) MD: Fe(011) GPP: (111) MD: Fe(111) Figure 7: Average surface stress vs. temperature for BCC metals Saxena et al.: Preprint submitted to Elsevier Page 13 of 20 Finite-temperature surface elasticity of crystalline solids W, and V (again showing only quarter or half polar plots by exploiting symmetries). Let us point out some speciﬁcs for each surface orientation (for all data, see Appendix B). The (001) BCC surface has 4mm symmetry, analogous to the FCC case. Our results and those by Grochola et al. [15] predict the surface stress for this face to be signiﬁcantly higher than those of the other faces. (Note that this is in contrast to the ab-initio data by Schönecker et al. [36], who reported 휏avg (011) > 휏avg (001).) The (011) surface of BCC metals belongs to the 2mm symmetry group. It is interesting to note that the Fe (011) surface shows 휏22 < 0, leading to a compressive surface stress at higher temperature, while the W (011) surface shows 휏22 < 0 at all temperatures (see Table 9). Also, 휏11 > 휏22 holds in general. This may seem counter-intuitive due to the atomic arrangement: every atom has two nearest neighbors on either side of the 푦-푧-plane under an angle of cos−1(1∕ √ 3) ≈55◦with respect to the 푥-axis, so that the stretch originating from these nearest neighbors have more eﬀect along the 푦-axis. However, two more nearest neighbors lie below the surface in the 푥-푧-plane (hence contributing to forces only along the 푥-direction) and two second-nearest neighbors on the surface along the 푥-axis, both of which lead to a stronger tensile stress along the 푥-direction. Finally, the (111) surface has 3m symmetry and is isotropic but lacks the closed-packed structure of the (111) FCC surface. Isotropy is veriﬁed via 2퐶1212 = 퐶1111 −퐶1122, which holds up to three signiﬁcant digits (see Table 10). Like for FCC metals, common trends of surface elastic constants with temperature are diﬃcult to identify for BCC metals. Figure 8: Polar compliance plots for BCC metals. Per the BCC surface symmetries, only one quarter/semi-circle of the full polar plot is shown for diﬀerent surface orientations, which can be rotated about the origin to visualize the full polar plots (see Table 1). All compliance values are in Å2∕푒푉. 5. Conclusion We have presented a computationally eﬃc",2204.13028v1,"cond-mat.mtrl-sci, cond-mat.mes-hall, cond-mat.stat-mech",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2204.13028v1,,b'\x1f\x85uB