)𝜀𝐾𝐿𝑛̂𝑁𝑑𝑆, (64) where 𝐧 is outward unit vector normal to surface (S) of 𝑉𝐗 and 𝐵̂𝐼𝐽𝑘𝑙= 𝐶̂𝐼𝐽𝑘𝑙+ 𝜎̂𝐼𝑙𝛿𝑘𝐽+ 𝜎̂𝑙𝐽𝛿𝑘𝐼−𝜎̂𝐼𝐽𝛿𝑘𝑙, (65) Λ̂𝑖𝑗𝐾𝐿𝑁= 𝑊̂𝑖𝑗𝐾𝐿𝑁+ 𝜏̂𝐾𝐿𝑗𝛿𝑖𝑁+ 𝜏̂𝐾𝑗𝑁𝛿𝑖𝐿+ 𝜏̂𝑗𝐿𝑁𝛿𝑖𝐾−𝜏̂𝐾𝐿𝑁𝛿𝑖𝑗. (66) Considering the expression of 𝛿𝑊 (See Eq.16) and neglecting effects of higher order strain gradients (i.e., terms containing 𝜂𝑖𝑗,𝑚𝑁 in the Eq.64), the equilibrium condition requires 𝛿𝐹̅ −𝛿𝑊̅ = 𝛿𝐹̅ −∫𝑡𝐼𝐽𝜀𝐼𝐽𝑑𝑉𝐗 = ∫[𝜎̂𝐼𝐽−𝜏̂𝐼𝐽𝑁,𝑁−𝑡𝐼𝐽+ (𝐵̂𝐼𝐽𝑘𝑙+ 𝜏̂𝐼𝐽𝑙,𝑘−Λ̂𝑘𝑙𝐼𝐽𝑁,𝑁)𝜂𝑘𝑙 + (𝑊̂𝐼𝐽𝑘𝑙𝑛−Λ̂𝑘𝑙𝐼𝐽𝑛−𝛱̂𝑘𝑙𝑛𝐼𝐽𝑁,𝑁)𝜂𝑘𝑙,𝑛]𝜀𝐼𝐽d𝑉𝐗 + ∫(𝜏̂𝐾𝐿𝑁+ Λ̂𝑖𝑗𝐾𝐿𝑁𝜂𝑖𝑗+ 𝛱̂𝑖𝑗𝑚𝐾𝐿𝑁𝜂𝑖𝑗,𝑚)𝜀𝐾𝐿𝑛̂𝑁𝑑𝑆= 0 (67) To make the last equality in (67) satisfied for arbitrary ε, the expression in the square bracket of the first integral should be zero, that is 𝑡𝑖𝑗= 𝜎̂𝑖𝑗−𝜏̂𝑖𝑗𝑚,𝑚+ (𝐵̂𝑖𝑗𝑘𝑙+ 𝜏̂𝐼𝐽𝑙,𝑘−Λ̂𝑘𝑙𝑖𝑗𝑁,𝑁)𝜂𝑘𝑙+ (𝑊̂𝑖𝑗𝑘𝑙𝑛−Λ̂𝑘𝑙𝑖𝑗𝑛−𝛱̂𝑘𝑙𝑛𝑖𝑗𝑁,𝑁)𝜂𝑘𝑙,𝑛. (68a) It is worth noting that 𝝈̂ and 𝝉̂ (as the other elastic constants) are measured at initial configuration, i.e., *𝒂+, while t is the extra stress balanced by the internal stresses of configuration *𝑿+. A special, but important, case is that the initial configuration is free of strain or strain gradient. In such case, we have 𝜎̂𝑖𝑗−𝜏̂𝑖𝑗𝑚,𝑚= 0 because of Eq. (37). Then the equilibrium equation is simplified to be 𝑡𝑖𝑗= (𝐵̂𝑖𝑗𝑘𝑙+ 𝜏̂𝐼𝐽𝑙,𝑘−Λ̂𝑘𝑙𝑖𝑗𝑁,𝑁)𝜂𝑘𝑙+ (𝑊̂𝑖𝑗𝑘𝑙𝑛−Λ̂𝑘𝑙𝑖𝑗𝑛−𝛱̂𝑘𝑙𝑛𝑖𝑗𝑁,𝑁)𝜂𝑘𝑙,𝑛, (68b) 11 which indicates that internal stresses are linearly changed with strain and strain gradients. For centrosymmetric crystals where 𝝉̂ and 𝑾̂ are zeros, Eq. (68b) becomes 𝑡𝑖𝑗= 𝐶̂𝑖𝑗𝑘𝑙𝜂𝑘𝑙−𝛱̂𝑘𝑙𝑛𝑖𝑗𝑁,𝑁𝜂𝑘𝑙,𝑛. (68c) If the crystals are free of strain gradient, Eq. (68c) will reduce to Hooke’s law. Specially, for small η, Eq. (68b) or (68c) should be equivalent to Eq. (37), which results in 𝜎̂𝑖𝑗(𝐗) = (𝐵̂𝑖𝑗𝑘𝑙+ 𝜏̂𝑖𝑗𝑙,𝑘−Λ̂𝑘𝑙𝑖𝑗𝑁,𝑁)𝜂𝑘𝑙≡𝐶̂𝑖𝑗𝑘𝑙(𝐗)𝜂𝑘𝑙, (69a) 𝜏̂𝑖𝑗𝑚,𝑚(𝐗) = −(𝑊̂𝑖𝑗𝑘𝑙𝑛−Λ̂𝑘𝑙𝑖𝑗𝑛−𝛱̂𝑘𝑙𝑛𝑖𝑗𝑁,𝑁)𝜂𝑘𝑙,𝑛≡𝛱̂𝑘𝑙𝑛𝑖𝑗𝑚,𝑚(𝐗)𝜂𝑘𝑙,𝑛. (69b) or 𝜎̂𝑖𝑗(𝐗) = 𝐶̂𝑖𝑗𝑘𝑙𝜂𝑘𝑙, 𝜏̂𝑖𝑗𝑚,𝑚(𝐗) = 𝛱̂𝑘𝑙𝑛𝑖𝑗𝑚,𝑚𝜂𝑘𝑙,𝑛. (70) In Eq. (69a) and (69b), the elastic constants at configuration *𝑿+ relate to the ones at *𝒂+ by 𝐶̂𝑖𝑗𝑘𝑙(𝐗) = 𝐵̂𝑖𝑗𝑘𝑙+ 𝜏̂𝑖𝑗𝑙,𝑘−Λ̂𝑘𝑙𝑖𝑗𝑛,𝑛, 𝛱̂𝑘𝑙𝑛𝑖𝑗𝑚,𝑚(𝐗) = −𝑊̂𝑖𝑗𝑘𝑙𝑛+ Λ̂𝑘𝑙𝑖𝑗𝑛+ 𝛱̂𝑘𝑙𝑛𝑖𝑗𝑛,𝑛. (71) Thus, the equilibrium equation (68b) or (68c) could expressed in configuration *𝑿+ by 𝑡𝑖𝑗= 𝜎̂𝑖𝑗(𝐗) −𝜏̂𝑖𝑗𝑚,𝑚(𝐗). (68d) In contrast to the strain-related elastic constitutive relation (69a), Eq. (69b) represents strain-gradient related elastic constitutive relation. The stability condition for finite strains requires 𝛿2𝐹̅ = ∫[(𝐵̂𝐼𝐽𝑘𝑙+ 𝜏̂𝐼𝐽𝑙,𝑘−Λ̂𝑘𝑙𝐼𝐽𝑁,𝑁)𝛿𝜂𝑘𝑙+ (𝑊̂𝐼𝐽𝑘𝑙𝑛−Λ̂𝑘𝑙𝐼𝐽𝑛−𝛱̂𝑘𝑙𝑛𝐼𝐽𝑁,𝑁)𝛿𝜂𝑘𝑙,𝑛]𝜀𝐼𝐽d𝑉𝐗> 0, (72) where the surface contribution is omitted since the surface only serves as a virtual bound used to distinguish the inner (materials of interest) for the outer. Furthermore, only bulk properties of solid are concerned here. Substituting (61) and (62) into above expression and retaining up to the second order small quantities of strains and strain gradients, we have 𝛿2𝐹̅ = ∫[(𝐵̂𝐼𝐽𝐾𝐿+ 𝜏̂𝐼𝐽𝐿,𝐾−Λ̂𝐾𝐿𝐼𝐽𝑁,𝑁)𝜀𝐾𝐿+ (𝑊̂𝐼𝐽𝐾𝐿𝑀−Λ̂𝐾𝐿𝐼𝐽𝑀−𝛱̂𝐾𝐿𝑀𝐼𝐽𝑁,𝑁)𝜀𝐾𝐿,𝑀]𝜀𝐼𝐽d𝑉𝐗 = ∫(𝐵̂𝐼𝐽𝐾𝐿+ 𝜏̂𝐼𝐽𝐿,𝐾−Λ̂𝐾𝐿𝐼𝐽𝑁,𝑁)𝜀𝐼𝐽𝜀𝐾𝐿d𝑉𝐗+ ∫(𝑊̂𝐼𝐽𝐾𝐿𝑀−Λ̂𝐾𝐿𝐼𝐽𝑀−𝛱̂𝐾𝐿𝑀𝐼𝐽𝑁,𝑁)𝜀𝐾𝐿,𝑀𝜀𝐼𝐽d𝑉𝐗. (73) From above equation, we could conclude that not only strain (represented by 𝐁̂), but also strain gradient (represented by 𝛕̂, 𝐖̂ (or 𝚲̂) and 𝚷̂), govern the mechanical stabilities of solids. And the 𝛕̂ and 𝐖̂ (or 𝚲̂) are nearly zero and thus be omitted for small strain gradients, contribution of the strain gradient on the mechanical stabilities mainly result from the second integral in Eq. (73) through 𝚷̂. Obviously, if −𝛱̂𝐾𝐿𝑀𝐼𝐽𝑁,𝑁 is positive definite, then result of the second integral will be positive. In other words, solids are stable under disturbances of strain gradients if 𝑇̂𝐾𝐿𝑀𝐼𝐽𝑁,𝑁 is negative definite. This conclusion has been justified in single crystal iron under ramp compressions with various strain rates. Because the Eq. (73) only reflects the separate effects of strain and strain gradient, respectively, total effects on the stabilities are still unknown. To make clear of the total effects, the second integral in the Eq. (73) is further simplified as follows: ∫(𝑊̂𝐼𝐽𝐾𝐿𝑀−Λ̂𝐾𝐿𝐼𝐽𝑀−𝛱̂𝐾𝐿𝑀𝐼